A||N||18||rigdonhome||Dream With Me !!!  Any Knowledge of Upcoming Front Wheel Drive 5/6-Speeds?||||3||
Z||000000||rigdonhome||11-17-2001||09:55 PM||||I absolutely have to have a manual transmission when I do my V8 swap...  However, I am planning to use an LS6 that (combined with my maniacal driving style) will undoubtedly be the death of even the best Fiero 5-speed.<P>The thought occurred to me today, however, that rumors are getting closer and closer to reality that the Impala/Monte Carlo chassis will see an LS1 V8 in the near future.  If this happens, I can only imagine that as horsepower in these platforms becomes more worthy of those 'SS' emblems, the type of people who buy them will want a manual transmission behind (underneath, I guess is the new-age version) the V8!<P>So, hypothetically speaking, if GM offered an LS1/6-speed Impala/Monte Carlo, I would jump at the chance to be the first to swap the combination into my '88 GT!  <P>Does anyone have any knowledge of any of the above dreaming/rumors actually becoming a reality?  Does anyone even have any better or more recent rumors than what I have?  Am I completely dreaming?  <P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000001||filthyscarecrow||11-17-2001||11:18 PM||||i heard the LS1 impala was a pipe dream...<P>i do know that the focus SVT has a 6 speed getrag FWD gearbox... yuo'd probably need an adapter plate to get it to bolt up, but how bad do you want 6 speeds? i think the ricers can put about 400hp (low 300's torque) through it no prob. i shoudl look into this a bit more...||||reg||1
Z||000002||loafer87gt||11-17-2001||11:53 PM||||What really excites me is the new GM v6 engines. The last issue of Motortrend had a short blurb on the new DOHC v6 engines that GM is going to be using in their lineup. Apparently, the 3.8 is going to be the only non overhead cam engine used in the new GM's. The one engine that really jumped out at me was the 321hp turbo DOHC 2.8 motor. The other motors that GM will be producing are a normally aspirated DOHC 2.8, DOPHC 3.0, and a DOHC 3.2. I will grab the magazine off of my buddy and scan it in for you guys, they sound like great potential engine swaps. It will be interesting to see what trannies they plan on using with these new motors, hopefully the 6 speed from out of the new Vibe.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||11-18-2001||12:14 AM||||new euro caddy catearia 03 will have a 6speed manual maybe getrag made, and a 5 speed auto and uses 3.0 v6 same as saab remmenber GM OWNS THEM uses with a turbo rated at 230 hp with a 5 speed manual now in a 2002 model.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||11-18-2001||03:50 AM||||LS1 front-driver? NO way.  <P>Name one V8 front-drive car with a manual tranny...  Torque-steer!<P><BR>Now, there are the 6-speeds from the Celica and Acura RSX...  but as for bolting them to an LS6?  I think a Fiero 282 Getrag has a far better chance of surviving.<P>Really, I'd just be worried about your clutch.  Archie's centreforce hasn't given much trouble to the 1500-odd V8 fieros running around out there, including many with more BHP than an LS6.<P>And if it really gets nasty, get a double (or  extend the splines on your input shaft and go TRIPPLE) plate Quartermaster clutch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>(these guys make the 3 and 4 puck IRL clutches.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000005||rigdonhome||11-18-2001||09:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by loafer87gt:<BR><B>What really excites me is the new GM v6 engines. The last issue of Motortrend had a short blurb on the new DOHC v6 engines that GM is going to be using in their lineup. Apparently, the 3.8 is going to be the only non overhead cam engine used in the new GM's. The one engine that really jumped out at me was the 321hp turbo DOHC 2.8 motor. The other motors that GM will be producing are a normally aspirated DOHC 2.8, DOPHC 3.0, and a DOHC 3.2. I will grab the magazine off of my buddy and scan it in for you guys, they sound like great potential engine swaps. It will be interesting to see what trannies they plan on using with these new motors, hopefully the 6 speed from out of the new Vibe.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>loafer87gt !!!<P>I'm looking forward to seeing those scans!  Do you know exactly what month of Motor Trend it was?  If it's still on the stands I'll go out and pick up a copy!<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000006||rigdonhome||11-18-2001||09:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>LS1 front-driver? NO way.  <P>Name one V8 front-drive car with a manual tranny...  Torque-steer!<P><BR>Now, there are the 6-speeds from the Celica and Acura RSX...  but as for bolting them to an LS6?  I think a Fiero 282 Getrag has a far better chance of surviving.<P>Really, I'd just be worried about your clutch.  Archie's centreforce hasn't given much trouble to the 1500-odd V8 fieros running around out there, including many with more BHP than an LS6.<P>And if it really gets nasty, get a double (or  extend the splines on your input shaft and go TRIPPLE) plate Quartermaster clutch   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>(these guys make the 3 and 4 puck IRL clutches.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ben!<P>I thought torque-steer was caused by the unequal length of the half-shafts.  The longer shaft will twist more than the shorter one, therefore causing one wheel to 'outrun' the other...  Theoretically, this should be amplified by torque and horsepower alone, not engine size or manual/auto trans...<P>However, you mentioned that V8 manual front-drivers are somehow more prone to torque-steer...  Why?  <P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||rigdonhome||11-18-2001||09:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by filthyscarecrow:<BR><B>i heard the LS1 impala was a pipe dream...<P>i do know that the focus SVT has a 6 speed getrag FWD gearbox... yuo'd probably need an adapter plate to get it to bolt up, but how bad do you want 6 speeds? i think the ricers can put about 400hp (low 300's torque) through it no prob. i shoudl look into this a bit more...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  A Ford transmission in my Fiero ???  Well, I guess if it's a Getrag, MAYBE...<P>I've seen enough LS1 Impala prototypes and heard enough about the demand for police versions that I have faith the Impala will see an LS1 soon.  Just my own opinion...  Caddy has had V8/front-drive cars for how long now?  Why not Chevy?<P><P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||loafer87gt||11-18-2001||01:18 PM||||I think it was the November issue with a bunch of trucks and a Trailblazer on the front cover. I can scan it in mid week, I just have to go grab the mag from my buddies place.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick's Dad||11-18-2001||03:52 PM||||Torque steer is not the reason that there are no FWD V8-5/6SP GM cars out there.<P>Correctly stated above, torque steer happens when one half shaft is longer than the other. The shorter one doesn't "wind up" as much, thereby turning it's wheel earlier, so the car steers itself in the direction of the still flexing longer halfshaft. Torque steer happens on 4 and 6 cyl cars, like my wife's Buick LeSabre. Of course, the Fiero is all but immune to this, because it's drive wheels are where they should be.<P>There are a gaggle of FWD V8's, mostly from Caddy, but also from Lincoln and others. These are all automatics, though, because of pulic demand. Caddy and Lincoln drivers especially (read: 60+ crowd) want slushboxes because they're not really drivers. A car is simple utility for them, or they tend to sit in traffic (where, I hate to admit, a clutch *can* get old). Since when was a DeVille "fun to drive?" Even Lincoln's LS's, which are RWD, only offer the manual with the V6. <P>BTW, the Catera, and it's replacement, the CTS, are RWD. The Opel version of the Catera is available with an LS1/6Sp. drivetrain. Caddy said that they'd sell "only about 1,000" Cateras so equipped here in the US, so it's not worth importing and getting approved.... Interestingly though, the CTS may come through like this, especially since Caddy has just created a "performance" group (C&D), and its looks are growing on me.<P>Edited to correct moniker of new Caddy.<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick's Dad (edited 11-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||avengador1||11-18-2001||05:00 PM||||How about this engine? Sorry if the picture is too big. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GM-V-8_1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||11-22-2001||11:14 AM||||Heh... Don't hold your breath waiting for GM to come out with a new FWD manual transaxle. If it ever happens, it won't be for years.<P>That aside, check out the six speed in the new Maxima. The factory puts it behind a 250 ftlbs engine, so it should take a V8 at least as well as the Getrag.<P>If you want to get really expensive, you could have <A HREF="http://www.eiptuning.com" TARGET=_blank>www.eiptuning.com</A>  build you an indestructible VW racing transaxle... for $5000.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rigdonhome||11-25-2001||06:32 PM||||I found some great info on this subject in a magazine I picked up the other day!  I'd never heard of it, but it's great, at least this edition!  It's called GM High Tech Performance.<P>The editorial is all about the current and future trends in GM performance vehicles!  I'll type in the chart at the bottom of the page that gives the bottom line, but the whole page is full of info so I scanned it in and provided a link.  The image is WAY too big to post!  <P>Basically, this is the 'in and out' list for GM's latest performance creations...<P>IN IN IN IN IN<BR>Chevrolet SSR, RWD, 2002<BR>Pontiac Vibe GT, FWD, 2003 (With 6-speed!!!)<BR>Cadillac XLR, RWD, 2003<BR>Cadillac CTSi, RWD, 2004<BR>Cadillac Seville STS, RWD, 2005<BR>Buick LaCross, RWD, 2006<P>MAYBE MAYBE MAYBE MAYBE<BR>Chevrolet Camaro, RWD, 2005<BR>V8 Impala SS, RWD, 2006<BR>V8 Monte Carlo, RWD, 2006<BR>Pontiac REV, AWD, TBD<BR>Pontiac GTO, RWD, TBD<BR>Chevrolet Borrego, AWD, TBD<BR>Cadillac Cien, RWD, TBD<P>NEVER NEVER NEVER<BR>Pontiac Firebird<BR>All J-cars (Cavaliers...)<P>What excites me even more than the 6-speed front-wheel drive Pontiac is the amount of rear while drive vehicles that are returning!  <P>The entire page is scanned in the image below:<P><A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/pennocks/gmhtp.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/pennocks/gmhtp.jpg</A> <P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>42,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||lou||11-25-2001||06:45 PM||||Since you are going to dream dream big. 550 hp V12 smaller and lighter than an LT1 with the electronics on the engine for a simple positive and negative connection .  Attached to a six speed clutchless manual with electronic paddle shifting on the steering wheel, traction control and user variable differential ratios.  A dream that will be a reality in 2003. <A HREF="http://www.thefierosite.com/gallery/NORTHV12.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thefierosite.com/gallery/NORTHV12.jpg</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.thefierosite.com/gallery/NORTHV12.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by lou (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pontiaddict||11-25-2001||07:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rigdonhome:<BR><B>Basically, this is the 'in and out' list for GM's latest performance creations...<P>IN IN IN IN IN<BR>Pontiac Vibe GT, FWD, 2003 (With 6-speed!!!)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Why wait for the Vibe to come out? Get the exact same transmission now in the Toyota Celica.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>MAYBE MAYBE MAYBE MAYBE<BR>Chevrolet Camaro, RWD, 2005<BR>V8 Impala SS, RWD, 2006<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <BR>Nope<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>V8 Monte Carlo, RWD, 2006<BR>Pontiac REV, AWD, TBD<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>doubt it<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>Pontiac GTO, RWD, TBD<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I hope not. Know that all of Pontiac's new designs are going to take their looks from the Aztec, just like the Vibe did.<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>Chevrolet Borrego, AWD, TBD<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Not likely, but look for similar styling for the new Tracker (or it's replacement).<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>Cadillac Cien, RWD, TBD<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Highly unlikely<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><P>NEVER NEVER NEVER<BR>Pontiac Firebird<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Never say never about the Firebird.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||rigdonhome||11-25-2001||07:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou:<BR><B>Since you are going to dream dream big. 550 hp V12 smaller and lighter than an LT1 with the electronics on the engine for a simple positive and negative connection .  Attached to a six speed clutchless manual with electronic paddle shifting on the steering wheel, traction control and user variable differential ratios.  A dream that will be a reality in 2003. <A HREF="http://www.thefierosite.com/gallery/NORTHV12.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thefierosite.com/gallery/NORTHV12.jpg</A> <P>[This message has been edited by lou (edited 11-25-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Where do I send the check???<P>Actually, I can also confirm through reliable sources that there WILL be a supercharged Northstar in 2006.  No word on how much HP...<P>I can't wait to see an engine swap that only takes a +/- connection!!!  Soon, soon.  With technology increasing at the rate it does, it's only a matter of time...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>42,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||rigdonhome||11-25-2001||07:25 PM||||Pontiaddict!<P>You seem pretty convinced that the Camaro and more specifically the V8 Impala SS (of any configuration) are not going to happen.  Where do you get your info?  <P>I'm not a GM executive or an Automotive Industry spy or anything, but I do read a lot and I was hearing about the 'New 2005 Camaro' long before the announcement was made to kill its current configuration in 2002.  From quite a ways back the plan has been to replace the Cadillac Eldorado and Camaro with a new platform similar to the Corvette with front engine/rear transaxle.  The information in the editorial I quoted seems to concur with this.<P>I'm not claiming that magazine articles should be trusted 100% for accuracy, but after years of similar projections it seems likely that there is some credibility to this particular article.  Working in the Product Design aspect Automotive Industry also gets you some more trustworthy gossip...<P>Do you have other sources for information that you can disclose?<P>And on the Firebird note, I shamefully retract my 'never' statement...  In fact, it was simply a quote as I said above, but I don't believe for a second that the Firebird is gone forever!  Let's just hope the name comes back on a vehicle worthy of the name, unlike the disgrace they put the Malibu name to...<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>42,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Pontiaddict||11-26-2001||09:20 PM||||Actually I was commenting on just the Impala, I should have just deleted the part about the Camaro.<P>As for the Impala. GM <I>was</I> going to build a fwd LS1 powered car. Then they decided that it wasn't justifiable and got rid of that idea. Then they were going to make all of their full size cars rear wheel drive and v8 powered. Apparantly they decided that wasn't the way to go either. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||11-28-2001||02:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou:<BR><B>Since you are going to dream dream big. 550 hp V12 smaller and lighter than an LT1 with the electronics on the engine for a simple positive and negative connection .  Attached to a six speed clutchless manual with electronic paddle shifting on the steering wheel, traction control and user variable differential ratios.  A dream that will be a reality in 2003. <P>[This message has been edited by lou (edited 11-25-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm not sure I want that engine...<BR>I think that they made too many compromises to packaging: The specific torque is absolutely pathetic for an engine of that calibre. The XV12 makes an anemic 61 ftlbs/litre, the new M3 engine makes over 82 ftlbs/litre. Specific torque is the true indicator of the quality of an engine's design: it takes into account cylinder head, combustion chamber, intake and exhaust design. Specific horsepower is not as good an indicator of engine design as it is easily influenced by RPM.<P>I'm not bashing GM.I think that the directions they are currently going are outstanding improvements over where they've been, but they're still riddled with compromises.||||reg||1
